My project brief

The question

Which local government areas in Abuja Municipal Area Council (AMAC) experience the highest Urban Heat Island intensity due to built‑up density and vegetation loss?


The data I need

-FCT LGA boundary shapefiles - GRID3 - https://data.grid3.org - GeoPackage - 50MB.
-Landsat 8/9 thermal infrared bands - USGS Earth Explorer - https://earthexplorer.usgs.gov - GeoTIFF - 919MB.
-Sentinel‑2 multispectral bands - Copernicus Open Access Hub - https://scihub.copernicus.eu - SAFE package (JPEG2000 inside) - 700MB.
-Digital Elevation Model (DEM) - CGIAR‑CSI - https://srtm.csi.cgiar.org - GeoTIFF, ASCII - 23MB.
-Road network shapefiles - Geofabrik - https://download.geofabrik.de - Shapefile, GeoPackage - 200MB.
-Hydrology layers - HydroSHEDS - https://www.hydrosheds.org - GeoTIFF (.tif), GeoPackage - 3GB.
-Population density data - WorldPop - https://www.worldpop.org - GeoTIFF (.tif), ASCII XYZ - 90MB.
-Meteorological records - NiMet Climate Data Portal - https://nimet.gov.ng or ENACTS datasets - NetCDF (.nc), ASCII, ArcInfo GRID - 1GB.


Why it matters

Understanding where heat intensity is strongest in AMAC is critical for urban planners, environmental agencies, public health officials and the Nigerian government as a whole. 
Planners can prioritize green infrastructure in hotspots, while health agencies can prepare cooling interventions for vulnerable or affected communities. 
Non-Governmental Organizations (NGOs) and climate researchers/advocates can use the insights to advocate for sustainable development policies. 
By pinpointing heat‑affected locations within the LGA, decision‑makers can allocate resources more effectively and reduce risks of heat stress, energy demand spikes and poor air quality.


What I would build

A web‑based interactive dashboard that displays NDVI, NDBI and LST layers per ward within AMAC, which would be updated monthly as new satellite data arrives. 
Users could toggle between vegetation, built‑up density and heat intensity maps, with pop‑ups showing average/max values per ward. 
The dashboard would serve as a decision‑support tool for planners and health agencies, helping them identify hotspots and track changes over time.

